    \end{minipage}\hfill
    \begin{minipage}[c]{0.30\linewidth}
        \captionsetup{justification=raggedright,singlelinecheck=false}
        \caption{ \textbf{A control bottleneck.} At state $2$, action $a_+$ enters cluster A (states $3,4$), while $a_-$ enters cluster B (states $5,6$). Both branches lead to the shared tail (states $7$--$10$). Reward is $1$ in cluster A and $0$ elsewhere, so $a_+$ has a higher return despite the overlap in the two actions' futures.
        }
        \label{fig:didactic-mdp}
    \end{minipage}
\end{figure}

\Cref{fig:didactic-mdp} shows the process used in our experiments. 
At state $2$, action $a_+$ enters the rewarded cluster A, while $a_-$ enters the unrewarded cluster B; both branches subsequently
reach the shared tail. 
Control therefore depends on retaining the distinction between the branches despite their overlapping futures.
\Cref{sec:exp-didactic} measures how well FB and \name{} preserve the resulting action-value difference.


\fan{Connecting paragraph} The analysis shows that a low-rank approximation can capture much of the future occupancy yet miss the difference that determines which action to take. Therefore, we need to have a representation that remains compact while allowing the predicted distributions to express action differences beyond those captured by a direct low-rank fit. 

